  line:
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: With a letter of introduction to a friend of Doctor Blumentritt in
      Vienna, Nordenfels, the greatest of Austrian novelists, we went on to the capital,
      where we were entertained by the Concordia Club. So favorable was the impression
      that I made upon Mr. Nordenfels that an answer was written to the note of introduction,
      thanking the professor for having brought to his notice a person whom he had
      found so companionable and whose genius he so much admired
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 0
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: Nordenfels had been interested in Spanish subjects, and was able to
      intelligently discuss the peculiar development of Castilian civilization and
      the politics of the Spanish metropolis as they affected the overseas possessions.
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 1
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: After having seen Rome and a little more of Italy, they embarked for
      the Philippines, again on the French mail, from Marseilles, coming by way of
      Saigon, where a rice steamer was taken for Manila.
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 1
  - speakerName: 
    speakerType: 2
    hero: {fileID: 0}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: 'Chapter V: A Rizal''s Tour of the World. End.'
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 1
